WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.500
Jumping right in, Project Compilation.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.719
Before we look at the minutia of what an Unreal project is,

3
00:00:07.719 --> 00:00:09.539
let's take a look at what we've got available to us

4
00:00:09.539 --> 00:00:11.980
when working with Compilation in the Unreal Engine.

5
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.140
The Unreal Engine provides custom building methods

6
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:17.230
via the Unreal Build Tool or the UBT.

7
00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.429
This unique tool will build

8
00:00:18.429 --> 00:00:19.890
the engine's reflection system.

9
00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.269
It will integrate any C++ code with blueprints.

10
00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:25.410
It'll deal with any replication backend

11
00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.620
and deal with serialization.

12
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.640
The Unreal Build Tool is capable of spinning up

13
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.079
a wide selection of build configurations

14
00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:33.520
that developers can leverage

15
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.560
during the development cycle of the project.

16
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.960
On the left, we have a sample of the configurations

17
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:39.960
that Unreal offers,

18
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.719
whilst on the right we have the targets

19
00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:43.890
that we can build to.

20
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.210
To elaborate further on the configurations,

21
00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:49.850
let's look at what each one offers us as engineers.

22
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.649
Debug, in this configuration,

23
00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:55.289
the entire engine and game code is unoptimized.

24
00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:57.649
This will be slower than any of the other configurations,

25
00:00:57.649 --> 00:00:59.289
but it will give us a lot more access

26
00:00:59.289 --> 00:01:01.390
to debug information.

27
00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:02.570
We've then got Debug Game,

28
00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.230
and this is similar to Debug,

29
00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.750
but only the game code is unoptimized.

30
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:07.989
To be more precise,

31
00:01:07.989 --> 00:01:09.750
this covers the code within our project,

32
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.650
but not the engine.

33
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.150
We've then got Development.

34
00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.170
This configuration enables all but the most

35
00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.129
time-consuming engine and game code optimizations.

36
00:01:19.129 --> 00:01:20.790
From there, we've got Test.

37
00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.950
This configuration optimizes the code,

38
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.930
but allows for debug console commands

39
00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.430
and testing tools to be used,

40
00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.459
such as stats and profiling tools.

41
00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:33.260
This configuration does not work with an editor target.

42
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:34.420
Finally, we've got Shipping,

43
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.780
and this is our fully optimized code with stats,

44
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.299
profiling tools, and console commands removed.

45
00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:42.700
And again, this does not work with editor targets.

46
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.920
These configurations are paired

47
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.540
with a particular target in mind,

48
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.519
which are listed on the right-hand side of this slide.

49
00:01:48.519 --> 00:01:50.040
We've got Client and Server.

50
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.200
These targets build exactly what you think.

51
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.200
It will compile your project for a client or for a server.

52
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.359
Client code is stripped away

53
00:01:57.359 --> 00:02:00.920
from any server-based targets and vice versa.

54
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.920
We can wrap sections of code with server code

55
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.959
or not with server code

56
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.879
to exclude code from particular deployments.

57
00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:10.120
We've then got Editor,

58
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.620
and this target is unique

59
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.259
in that it will compile for all targets,

60
00:02:13.259 --> 00:02:16.169
and we'll discuss this in more depth soon.

61
00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.689
We've then got an Empty target,

62
00:02:17.689 --> 00:02:20.210
and this is a standalone target for single-player games.

63
00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.650
It'll be a single standalone game.

64
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.490
And this will include both server and client code.

65
00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.770
Any code that is preprocessed, as we mentioned,

66
00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.810
will be included in this build.

67
00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:32.469
Just a few notes on the Editor itself.

68
00:02:32.469 --> 00:02:33.750
The Editor is far more

69
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.310
than just a scripting tool for other teams.

70
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.030
Aside from providing a staggering array

71
00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.229
of environment authoring tools and asset setup frameworks,

72
00:02:41.229 --> 00:02:42.830
it allows developers to test their project

73
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.469
in both a single and a multiplayer environment.

74
00:02:45.469 --> 00:02:47.550
This is why the Editor target is so unique.

75
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.189
It allows us to compile every target

76
00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:50.210
that we could possibly want,

77
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.110
and allows us to use them all simultaneously.

78
00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.430
The Play and Editor features

79
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.430
can spin up more than one user and a dedicated server,

80
00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:59.430
all from within the Editor itself,

81
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.229
giving us a fantastic and easily accessible representation

82
00:03:02.229 --> 00:03:04.580
of our project post deploy.

83
00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.900
It's a common misconception

84
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.300
that Blueprint is for scripting and for prototyping.

85
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.979
This simply isn't the case.

86
00:03:09.979 --> 00:03:12.539
Entire projects can and have been made in Blueprint alone

87
00:03:12.539 --> 00:03:13.939
with no real downsides,

88
00:03:13.939 --> 00:03:16.199
because it's a tool much like anything else.

89
00:03:16.199 --> 00:03:18.539
If using Blueprint suits your studio's needs,

90
00:03:18.539 --> 00:03:20.099
then it absolutely should be considered

91
00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:22.139
as a primary implementation.

92
00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:25.300
Projects can be written in both C++ and Blueprint

93
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.539
using the Epic Launcher version of the engine,

94
00:03:27.539 --> 00:03:29.930
so you don't need the source.

95
00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.030
Whilst having access to the source is fantastic,

96
00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.650
it's entirely optional.

97
00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:35.930
We can grab the engine from the Epic Games Launcher

98
00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.610
and dive right in.

99
00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:38.930
The Epic Launcher version of the engine

100
00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.250
is simply a pre-compiled version of that source.

101
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:43.849
It's worth noting that we won't have access

102
00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:45.949
to the source symbols by default, however.

103
00:03:45.949 --> 00:03:48.449
They will need to be downloaded separately.

104
00:03:48.449 --> 00:03:50.389
If you'd like to have access to the source code,

105
00:03:50.389 --> 00:03:52.349
however, it is freely available.

106
00:03:53.229 --> 00:03:54.909
It's available on the Unreal Git repo

107
00:03:54.909 --> 00:03:57.830
and through the Unreal Perforce if you're a licensee.

108
00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:01.389
It's very useful if you intend to make engine changes.

109
00:04:01.389 --> 00:04:02.550
The symbols for the editor, however,

110
00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.270
can be downloaded from the launcher as pictured.
